__pycache__/

# DB dry-run copies (used by handoff doc commands)
data/.test-copy.db

# working-copy pilot DBs (throwaway; canonical DB is data/guidebook.db)
working/pilot/*.db

# transient SQLite sidecars from read-only WAL-mode connections (never work product;
# the canonical DB is committed as a single binary)
data/guidebook.db-shm
data/guidebook.db-wal

# derived structural-audit graph (throwaway build artifact; rebuilt each run by
# scripts/audit/graph_audit.py; never migration-tracked, never canonical)
data/audit_graph.db
data/audit_graph.db-shm
data/audit_graph.db-wal
# -journal, not only -wal: DR-2026-08-19 §12.0's F4 fix deleted PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL
# from db.py connect(), so these stores run in delete mode now and drop a -journal
# sidecar mid-run. This list was written for the WAL era and was one sidecar short, so
# every graph_audit run left an untracked file in git status — noise that teaches its
# reader to stop reading git status, which is the same disease as a check that is red by
# construction (CLAUDE.md rule 6).
data/audit_graph.db-journal

# Scratch databases AND THEIR SIDECARS. A batch copies data/guidebook.db into its
# session folder to write against (CLAUDE.md §4: scratch copy -> db.py -> emit ->
# migrate), and a `git add -A` issued before the migration is emitted sweeps that
# ~4 MB copy into the commit. The delta reaches main through the migration; the
# scratch copy is a working file and never belongs in history.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-09-18, twice over, and both corrections matter.
#
# (1) The glob was `*.db`, which does not match the `-journal`, `-wal` and `-shm`
#     sidecars. db.py's connect() no longer sets journal_mode=WAL, so these stores
#     run in delete mode and drop a `-journal` beside the scratch copy mid-run --
#     exactly the reason data/audit_graph.db-journal had to be listed above. The
#     rule would have caught the database and left its journal. `*.db*` catches both.
#
# (2) The first version of this comment said the mistake "happened in batch 16 and
#     again in batch 17". THAT IS FALSE and a code review caught it:
#     `git rev-list --all --objects | grep -E '\.db$'` lists exactly ONE scratchpad
#     blob across every ref, added in ca238cb by batch 17. Batch 16 removed its copy
#     with `git rm --cached` BEFORE committing, so it never entered history. A
#     hand-typed historical claim, wrong when written, in a governance file --
#     CLAUDE.md rule 7a, which is the rule this file was being edited to honour.
scratchpad/**/*.db*
